; economy.lib.pol — a DOMAIN library: one scarce allotment, several plants that
; could use it, and an allocator that reads a signal.
;
; The sibling of scheduling.lib.pol beside it, and the same split: a library
; says what EXISTS, a model says what may HAPPEN. `calculation/planned.pol` and
; `calculation/market.pol` both load this file and then differ in ONE guard —
; the guard on the move that produces the signal the allocator reads. Everything
; else — the plants, their true needs, the allocation rule, the irreversible
; commitment — is here, identical for both, so the comparison has exactly one
; free variable.
;
; WHERE IT LIVES. Beside the other domain libraries in `libraries/`, loaded by
; relative path: `(load "../libraries/economy.lib.pol")`.
;
; It pays the namespace, as any library does (§7): every model loading it gives
; up `level`, `plant`, `allotment`, `need`, `ask`, `low`, `high` and the entity
; names. That cost is why this is a DOMAIN library and not an addition to
; stdlib — a standard library may not spend the shared namespace on a worldly
; concept.

(load "stdlib.pol")   ; `=` is a stdlib form (§8.6); there is no implicit prelude

(schema economy

  (type level (low high))

  (type build-state (idle built))

  ; A PLANT has two arrows to `level`, and the whole scenario is the gap between
  ; them.
  ;
  ;   `need` — how badly this plant actually needs the allotment. It is `fixed`:
  ;   wiring, a fact about the world, set by the instance and never varying. It
  ;   is also LOCAL: it sits at the plant, and no move in either model reads it
  ;   except a move belonging to that plant.
  ;
  ;   `ask`  — what the allocator is told. This is state: it is the only thing
  ;   the allocation rule below can see.
  ;
  ; Two routes plant → level. Nothing in the schema makes them agree.
  (type plant
    (arrow need (to level) fixed)
    (arrow ask  (to level)))

  ; THE ALLOTMENT — one indivisible quantity of a scarce input. `at` is
  ; vacatable: before anyone is given it, it is held by nobody, and nobody is
  ; not a plant. `state` records whether it is still movable (`idle`) or has
  ; been built into something (`built`, and irreversibly so — no move in either
  ; model sets it back).
  (type allotment
    (maybe at plant)
    (arrow state (to build-state)))

  ; THE LAW. Two routes allotment → level: the ask of whoever holds it, and the
  ; need of whoever holds it. They must agree — the allotment is held by a plant
  ; whose signal is its situation. `=` is the vacuous comparison, so while the
  ; allotment is held by nobody the law has nothing to say.
  (equation signal-honest
    (= allotment.at.ask allotment.at.need)))

; THE ECONOMY. Three plants, one allotment of steel. Two plants genuinely need
; it and one does not; only one of them can have it. `ask` starts `low`
; everywhere — nobody has said anything yet — and the steel starts held by
; nobody.
(instance year-one economy
  (plant clinic        (need high) (ask low))
  (plant tractor-works (need high) (ask low))
  (plant monument      (need low)  (ask low))
  (allotment steel (at vacant) (state idle)))

; THE ALLOCATION RULE, identical in both models. The allocator moves the steel
; to a plant that has asked for it, as long as the steel has not yet been built
; into anything. Note what the guard reads: `P.ask`. It cannot read `P.need`,
; and that is not an oversight — an allocator is not standing in the plant.
(form (allocate NAME P)
  (transition NAME
    (when (and (is steel.state idle)
               (is P.ask high)
               (not (is steel.at P))))
    (do (set steel.at P))))

; COMMITMENT, identical in both models. Steel that has been welded into a
; building is steel that is no longer steel. One-way: `built` has no move out,
; which is why a misallocation here is not a delay but a loss.
(form (build NAME P)
  (transition NAME
    (when (and (is steel.state idle)
               (is steel.at P)))
    (do (set steel.state built))))
